   "title": "A graph that grows itself: what a knowledge graph is worth when nobody is there to query it",
   "study": {
    "title": "Can a system find connections in its own records that nobody asked it to find?",
    "authors": [
     "Ultrastable R&D"
    ],
    "institution": "Ultrastable",
    "kind": "own R&D question set, read against the published work on graph retrieval and link prediction and regenerated from this system's own graph",
    "version": "2026-11-25",
    "data": "The core knowledge graph this system rebuilds every day from its own work items, studies and rules (ops/kgraph), and the leads it files and settles (ops/kgraph/leads.jsonl)",
    "summary": "The common view is that a knowledge graph is a search aid: you build it, then someone queries it. Microsoft's GraphRAG work (Edge and others, 2024) showed graphs help most on questions about a whole corpus, by grouping entities into communities and summarising each one. LightRAG and HippoRAG pushed the same idea toward cheaper, incremental updates. Link prediction and Swanson's old literature-based discovery go further: the useful output is the missing edge. Ashby's ultrastable system needs exactly that kind of signal, a measure that tells the second loop when the first one is stuck. So we built the graph into every system Ultrastable runs. Each tick reads only rows past a cursor, pulls out ids, rule tags and file paths without a model, then has a small model name the entities and relations in those new rows, embeds them, and summarizes each community whose members changed. The spend throttle slows this down; it never switches it off. A pair that reads alike, has no edge and shares no neighbour becomes a lead. Two leads a day at most become work items. A lead counts as kept only when a later change links the pair for real."
   },
   "abstract": "Ultrastable R&D (Ultrastable): The common view is that a knowledge graph is a search aid: you build it, then someone queries it. Microsoft's GraphRAG work (Edge and others, 2024) showed graphs help most on questions about a whole corpus, by grouping entities into communities and summarising each one. LightRAG and HippoRAG pushed the same idea toward cheaper, incremental updates. Link prediction and Swanson's old literature-based discovery go further: the useful output is the missing edge. Ashby's ultrastable system needs exactly that kind of signal, a measure that tells the second loop when the first one is stuck. So we built the graph into every system Ultrastable runs. Each tick reads only rows past a cursor, pulls out ids, rule tags and file paths without a model, then has a small model name the entities and relations in those new rows, embeds them, and summarizes each community whose members changed. The spend throttle slows this down; it never switches it off. A pair that reads alike, has no edge and shares no neighbour becomes a lead. Two leads a day at most become work items. A lead counts as kept only when a later change links the pair for real. Most graph work assumes a person asks a question and the graph helps answer it. We test the other use: a system that builds the graph from everything it writes down, looks for pairs that read alike but are not linked, files each one as work, and counts how many turn out to be real. 1 of 1 are met today; each one that is not is an open work item with a closing probe, and this paper is regenerated from the measurements after every merge.",
